  {
   "id": "adr-decision-text",
   "type": "text",
   "x": 46,
   "y": 1255.0,
   "width": 1488,
   "height": 260.0,
   "angle": 0,
   "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
   "backgroundColor": "transparent",
   "fillStyle": "solid",
   "strokeWidth": 2,
   "strokeStyle": "solid",
   "roughness": 0,
   "opacity": 100,
   "groupIds": [],
   "frameId": null,
   "roundness": null,
   "seed": 1272189415,
   "version": 1,
   "versionNonce": 2023913385,
   "isDeleted": false,
   "boundElements": [],
   "updated": 1,
   "link": null,
   "locked": false,
   "text": "We choose B: the evidence check is generic code, and what a trial must report is a requirement set, a data file in src/trialwatch/checks/requirements/. Option B keeps\nrules under review and tests (risk) while removing every registry and regulation from the code and the prompt (quality), and every run logs the exact set it used\n(traceability).\n1. A requirement set names the document kinds to read, its requirements and how they combine into parts and a label. A requirement is fixed (one question) or one per\nitem of the registered information (e.g. each primary endpoint), with a question template, search hints and a rule type from a small vocabulary in code (count stated,\nstatement present, numeric result), plus the evidence kinds that mean \"not reported\".\n2. The prompt is generic: find, for each requirement, the passage in these documents that answers it, quote it, say where and what kind of evidence it is. The trial,\nits documents (titles, kinds, sources) and the questions are given per call, from data.\n3. Each judgement records its inputs at that moment: the trial record and the document versions read. Each run records the prompt and requirement texts it used, with\ntheir fingerprints. Parts are stored by name (jsonb), not as columns of one standard.\n4. No versioning before production: one current prompt, rule set and requirement set (no .v1 files, no rules version). Gold datasets stay versioned. Runs keep the\nexact texts, so nothing is lost when a file changes.\nThe first set, eu-ctr-results, reproduces today's questions and rules (Bruckner et al.'s reading of the EU Clinical Trials Regulation).",
   "originalText": "We choose B: the evidence check is generic code, and what a trial must report is a requirement set, a data file in src/trialwatch/checks/requirements/. Option B keeps\nrules under review and tests (risk) while removing every registry and regulation from the code and the prompt (quality), and every run logs the exact set it used\n(traceability).\n1. A requirement set names the document kinds to read, its requirements and how they combine into parts and a label. A requirement is fixed (one question) or one per\nitem of the registered information (e.g. each primary endpoint), with a question template, search hints and a rule type from a small vocabulary in code (count stated,\nstatement present, numeric result), plus the evidence kinds that mean \"not reported\".\n2. The prompt is generic: find, for each requirement, the passage in these documents that answers it, quote it, say where and what kind of evidence it is. The trial,\nits documents (titles, kinds, sources) and the questions are given per call, from data.\n3. Each judgement records its inputs at that moment: the trial record and the document versions read. Each run records the prompt and requirement texts it used, with\ntheir fingerprints. Parts are stored by name (jsonb), not as columns of one standard.\n4. No versioning before production: one current prompt, rule set and requirement set (no .v1 files, no rules version). Gold datasets stay versioned. Runs keep the\nexact texts, so nothing is lost when a file changes.\nThe first set, eu-ctr-results, reproduces today's questions and rules (Bruckner et al.'s reading of the EU Clinical Trials Regulation).",
   "fontSize": 16,
   "fontFamily": 6,
   "textAlign": "left",
   "verticalAlign": "top",
   "containerId": "adr-decision",
   "lineHeight": 1.25,
   "autoResize": true
  },
